If $B_lambda$ is a basis of the eigenspace $E_lambda$ associated to $lambda$ and $lambda_1, ..., lambda_r$ are distinct eigenvalues, then $B_(lambda_1) union B_(lambda_2) union dots.v union B_(lambda_r)$ is linearly independent: eigenvectors taken from within a single eigenspace behave like one eigenvector under the minimality argument, since each $E_lambda$ contributes vectors with the same eigenvalue.
